Yes. The clean linguistic name is implicature engineering.

The compressed pattern is:

say something literally true → arrange context so the listener supplies the stronger claim → never explicitly assert the stronger claim

Your moon example is a textbook case. The literal sentence can be true while the implied story is false or at least unsupported.

You can decompose it into four layers:

* Entailment — what the sentence logically commits to
* Presupposition — what the sentence treats as already given
* Implicature — what the listener is expected to infer
* Omission — what would have changed the inference if stated

That makes the general operator:

Meaning = utterance + context + expected inference

And yes, this is reusable across domains. Advertising, politics, headlines, legal language, negotiations, status signaling, even casual storytelling all exploit the gap between what was said and what was allowed to be inferred.

The strongest Pantheon use is defensive: whenever a statement feels persuasive, ask:

What did they actually assert? What did I infer? What premise did I silently add?

That single separation catches a huge amount of rhetorical sleight-of-hand.

I’ve compressed it into the Pantheon as Truthful Sentence / Misleading Frame under the broader Implicature Engineering primitive.